Kimigayo OS: floatscan out-of-bounds write

floatscan: when the circular mantissa buffer wraps, 0 is stored at
index -1. Reachable through strtold() and scanf %Lf with a crafted
number on both x86_64 (80-bit long double) and aarch64 (IEEE quad). Not
in a musl release as of 2026-10-14.

Source: musl git https://git.musl-libc.org/cgit/musl/commit/?id=dd856098ad76
  fetched from git.musl-libc.org on 2026-10-14.
The original message follows unchanged.

From dd856098ad76451718161f864158a38157d8b871 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rich Felker <dalias@aerifal.cx>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 15:36:08 -0400
Subject: floatscan: fix oob store at -1 index in mantissa buffer at wrap

the code to implement a modular postincrement was too clever, and
essentially did it as a reducing version of (z+=1)-1, but only applied
the redution after the addition not the subtraction. as a result, it
evaluated to -1 when wrapping, causing a fixed value of zero to be
stored out of bounds below the buffer.

whether this condition is triggered depends on where the mantissa bits
end up in the circular buffer after processing.

for archs with 80-bit extended long double or IEEE quad long double,
the input "710542735760100185871124267578125e-33" was reported to and
has been confirmed to trigger it.
---
 src/internal/floatscan.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/internal/floatscan.c b/src/internal/floatscan.c
index b15366b6..2bc6830f 100644
--- a/src/internal/floatscan.c
+++ b/src/internal/floatscan.c
@@ -256,7 +256,7 @@ static long double decfloat(FILE *f, int c, int bits, int emin, int sign, int po
 
 	/* Assemble desired bits into floating point variable */
 	for (y=i=0; i<LD_B1B_DIG; i++) {
-		if ((a+i & MASK)==z) x[(z=(z+1 & MASK))-1] = 0;
+		if ((a+i & MASK)==z) x[z] = 0, z = z+1 & MASK;
 		y = 1000000000.0L * y + x[a+i & MASK];
 	}
 
